      stat2: "edge + aftercare",
      "stat2.caption": "your arc needs both: the climb, then the landing",
      "edu.body.p3":
        "There's a third system underneath both: pleasure, how good it actually feels once it's happening. Arousal doesn't guarantee pleasure, and neither does desire. A body can be switched on while the experience stays empty or tense, and something quiet can feel deeply good with little physical charge. Pleasure integrates safety, presence, and meaning, so it answers a question of its own: does this feel good to me, overall?",
      "edu.body.p4":
        "Your body reacting is not the same as wanting, liking, or consenting. Scientists call this arousal non-concordance. A body can respond to things you don't want and would never choose. It isn't a flaw or a hidden truth about you; the arousal system is just fast and broad, and meaning gets added later, if at all. Reading a bodily reaction as proof of desire is where a lot of unnecessary shame begins.",
      "learn.eyebrow": "What you will learn",
      "learn.body":
        "In this chapter you will learn the difference between wanting, arousal, and pleasure, and why they do not always agree. Which order yours runs, and why a body reacting is not the same as wanting.",
