      "practical.line2":
        "2. Say the shutdown out loud once, in daylight: \"When things get vague or chaotic, my desire drops out. It's not about you. It's the confusion.\"",
      "practical.line3":
        '3. Turn the guessing into a request: "Just tell me straight what you want." A partner can answer a plain question. Nobody can answer a silent one.',
      "body.p1":
        "Under it all, your system scans for one thing: does this make sense? A hint instead of a request, a mixed signal, a mood that keeps shifting, these land early and hard, often before you consciously feel anything. That's why not knowing can close your desire faster than a flat no would. This is a detector, not a defect. It catches confusion that others shrug off, which is why people misread you as cold or too much in your head. The fix is never to switch it off. It's to say out loud what it needs, so the moment turns clear instead of murky.",
