        "A handful of belief clusters show up again and again. None is right or wrong:\n• Pleasure and outcome: sex is about enjoyment, release, novelty.\n• Validation and approval: being wanted proves worth.\n• Power and control: sex sets safety or hierarchy.\n• Relational and bonding: sex creates closeness and reassurance.\n• Service and other: sex is about pleasing the partner and keeping harmony.\n• Exploration and growth: sex is a place to learn and expand.\n• Stability and safety: sex belongs to routine and commitment.\n• Avoidant or defensive: sex feels risky, so it stays distant or minimized.\nEach began as an adaptive response to an early environment. Trouble starts only when a belief goes rigid, stays unconscious, or clashes with a partner's.",
      "body.p1":
        "Yours formed in places where love felt safest when nothing moved: a home, or an early relationship, where routine and loyalty were how you kept people close, or where instability taught you that sameness meant safety. None of them are wrong. Held too tightly, they turn ordinary change into a threat, so a partner wanting to try something new lands as \"I'm not enough,\" and a body braced for loss can't loosen into wanting.",
