    fantasy: {
      "gate.hook": "Your erotic map: what to lean into, what to keep in imagination",
      "edu.eyebrow": "Learn: what fantasies are for",
      "edu.teaser":
        "Most fantasies aren't wishes. The mind uses them for excitement, not as instructions. Knowing which of yours are fuel and which are actual desires changes everything about how to use the map above.",
      "edu.body.p1":
        "Fantasies serve at least three jobs: rehearsal (safely exploring what you might actually want), compensation (imagining what daily life lacks, such as control, surrender, or being pursued), and pure symbol (arousing as an image, unwanted as a reality). That's why a strong pull with low lived pleasure isn't hypocrisy; it's a symbol doing its job in the only place it works.",
      "edu.body.p2":
        "It helps to treat a fantasy less as a script to act out and more as a setting with its own rules. Each one carries quiet conditions: how visible am I, how much is at risk, who holds power, what happens afterward. Change those conditions and the same scene means something completely different, which is why a fantasy can feel alive in your head and wrong in your actual bed.",
      chartnote1:
        "Hover or tap any dot for its name and note. Placements start from your archetype's typical pattern; in the product you drag a dot to correct it, and the map becomes yours.",
      chartnote2:
        "Scaling note: label positions are data per archetype. Each report ships its own 8-label set (one per quadrant minimum), always placed below the dot for consistency.",
      "edu.body.p3":
        "This is also why a fantasy can feel hotter with a stranger than with a partner. A partner remembers you tomorrow, so some material stays erotic only where it doesn't have to be integrated into a shared identity. The pull can be real in the body while the mind wants none of it in reality. That gap between body and mind is normal, not a betrayal of who you love.",
      "edu.body.p4":
        'So the useful question isn\'t "should I live this," but: is this arousing, actually desired, or both? Would it still appeal if you imagined the whole thing, including what comes after? Would living it expand you, or just release something better left imagined? Lean into where the pull and the pleasure agree, try the hidden gems gently, and let imagination-only themes stay exactly there, guilt-free.',
      "learn.eyebrow": "What you will learn",
      "learn.body":
        "In this chapter you will learn what your fantasies are actually for, and why a strong pull in the mind can mean little in the body. What to lean into, and what is better left imagined.",
    },
